#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:1
msgid "How formats are named"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:3
msgid ""
"A format name such as `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.A` is a path down a tree. Each "
"segment narrows the one before, and the segments are ordered by how "
"likely a change in them is to change the shape of the PDF. This page is "
"the prescription that names are expected to follow, the reasoning behind "
"it, and — first, because it is the question the whole convention exists "
"to answer — how to use it when you have a document and do not know which "
"format reads it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:9
msgid "I have a document: which format do I use?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:11
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The situation is common. The document's URL is not in the repository's "
"`url_mapping.csv`, the repository's own classification ({doc}`document-"
"classify-yaml`) does not recognise it, and nobody has told you its "
"format. The names are built so that you can guess, and so that your "
"guesses come in a sensible order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "The heuristic, in one paragraph"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:19
msgid ""
"Rebuild the name the document would have — **entity, language, year, and "
"place of distribution if it says one** — from what its first and last "
"pages show. Then try the formats whose names agree with it, from the most"
" specific agreement to the least: every variant and every place under the"
" same entity, language and year first; then the same entity and language "
"in other years, nearest year below yours first; then outward. Stop at the"
" first format whose output is right. If the repository runs out, the "
"document needs a new format, and the one that came closest is where it "
"starts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:28
msgid "Step by step:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:30
msgid ""
"**Rebuild the name.** Read off the four segments described in [the "
"segments](#the-segments): the entity named at the start of the document, "
"the language it is written in, its year of publication, and — only if the"
" document addresses a particular country — that country."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:33
msgid ""
"**Same entity, language and year.** Try every format that differs from "
"your rebuilt name only in the place or the variant: `FINECO-EN23@LUX` and"
" `FINECO-EN23@IR`; `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.A` and `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.B`. The "
"variant carries no meaning you could read off the document ([below](#the-"
"segments)), so there is nothing to choose between variants except by "
"trying them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:37
msgid ""
"**Same entity and language, another year.** A name carries the "
"**earliest** year its recipe reads ([the year rule](#one-name-per-recipe-"
"and-the-smallest-year)), so a recipe named for an earlier year is the one"
" that may already extend to yours. For a 2022 document, with `EURIZON-"
"EN21` and `EURIZON-EN25` in the repository, try `EN21` first and `EN25` "
"second. If `EN25` turns out to read 2022, that is news worth reporting: "
"the format should be renamed `EURIZON-EN22`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:42
msgid ""
"**Further out.** The same entity in another language; then entities whose"
" names sit next to it. The odds fall with each step, and at this distance"
" a format that works is less a find than a sign that two recipes might be"
" one — something the repository's maintainers will want to hear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:45
msgid ""
"**Nothing works.** The document needs a format of its own, and it is "
"named by the rules [below](#naming-a-new-format). The format that came "
"closest in the search is the one to copy from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:48
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When the repository's own classification gets part of the way — it knows "
"the entity and the language but cannot tell the variant — the engine's "
"{doc}`classify <../cli/freeports-classify>` command prints that partial "
"name together with the formats of the repository it still allows. That "
"list *is* step 2 or 3 of this heuristic, already worked out for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:53
msgid "The segments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:59
msgid ""
"Read as a tree, `[entity]/[language]/[year]/[@place]?/[.variant]?`, the "
"way a file system would list it. Entity, language and year are always "
"present; place and variant are optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Segment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Written"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Means"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "entity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "any characters except `-`, `@`, `.`, `*` and spaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"the name the document is identified by — in practice the group behind the"
" funds, as named at the start of the document"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`MEDIOLANUM`, `ANIMA_SGR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "language"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "two capital letters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the language the document is written in"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`EN`, `IT`, `ES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "year"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "two digits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the year of publication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`23`, `24`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "place"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`@` and two or three capital letters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the country the report is addressed to, when there is one"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`@LUX`, `@IR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "variant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`.` and anything without `.` or `/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "nothing: it only tells apart two recipes no other segment separates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`.A`, `.B`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:70
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"A repository never writes these names out: `metadata/formats.csv` lists "
"the five segments in columns, and the name is synthesised from them "
"({doc}`../../guides/formats/repository`). So a name and its parts cannot "
"disagree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:74
msgid "The order: what is most likely to change the PDF"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:76
msgid ""
"The segments are ordered by the **supposed probability that a change in "
"that segment changes the shape of the PDF**. That is a hypothesis about "
"how reports are produced, stated as one; it was chosen because it is "
"plausible, not because it was measured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Why it sits there"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"An issuer brings everything that shapes a document at once — its "
"expertise and habits, the software that typesets its PDFs, the firm it "
"outsources the work to, its internal policies. A different entity is the "
"most likely source of a different layout, so it is the first cut."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"Changing language changes every label, which matters to any recipe that "
"reads labels. In principle it need not change the structure, so it comes "
"after the entity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"Everything the entity contributes, and the regulation it answers to, "
"drifts over time. Over several years that drift reaches the structure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"A report produced for a different audience may differ in detail or "
"emphasis, rarely in shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"Not a property of the document at all. It is there because there is "
"exactly one recipe per name, and some recipes differ for technical "
"reasons nothing on the cover reveals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:88
msgid "The three criteria the names answer to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:90
msgid "The segments were chosen by asking three things of a naming scheme:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:92
msgid ""
"**Names that are close must mean recipes that are close.** Sorted "
"alphabetically, as a file listing sorts them, similar documents should "
"end up next to each other and different ones far apart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:95
msgid ""
"**The rules for giving a name must be simple and unambiguous**, so that "
"two people naming the same document arrive at the same name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:97
msgid ""
"**What the name is made of must be evident in the document** — on its "
"first or last pages, present, and not open to interpretation. A segment "
"that could only be filled by reading the whole report would make the name"
" useless for the question at the top of this page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:101
msgid ""
"The first criterion is what gives the names their use beyond "
"identification. If names are given well, two documents that differ only "
"in their last segment are cousins, and two that differ in their entity "
"are strangers. That gives the work on recipes a **locality**: when trying"
" to merge two recipes, try neighbours first and distant names later; when"
" one recipe has to be split, the split falls at the segment where the "
"documents actually diverge. The name becomes an account of *why* two "
"documents look alike or do not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:108
msgid ""
"The third criterion is where the convention is weakest in practice. The "
"entity is meant to be the evident name at the start, but the name a "
"reader's colleagues use for the group is not always the largest word on "
"the cover: in the repository, a cover reading `WILLERFUNDS` is a "
"`FIDEURAM` format, `CHALLENGE FUNDS` a `MEDIOLANUM` one, `SIDERA FUNDS` a"
" `CARNE` one. When rebuilding a name, if the cover's own name finds "
"nothing, the name of the management company printed further in is the "
"next thing to try."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:115
msgid "One name per recipe, and the smallest year"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:117
msgid ""
"**Names follow the recipe, not the document.** There is exactly one "
"recipe per name, and a document is filed under the name of the recipe "
"that reads it — whatever its own year or entity says. A 2022 document "
"read by `EURIZON-EN21` is an `EURIZON-EN21` document; it does not earn a "
"new name for being from 2022."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:122
msgid "Two rules settle which name a recipe carries when its documents disagree:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "When a recipe reads documents…"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "It is named for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Why"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "of several years"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the **earliest** of them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"The search above tries the nearest earlier year first. A recipe named for"
" its first year is found by everyone whose document is from that year or "
"later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "of several entities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the **shortest** entity name; at equal length, the **most widely known**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid ""
"To maximise the chance that the name coincides with what the user reads "
"on their own document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:129
msgid ""
"It follows that a rename is a normal event. When a recipe named `EN25` is"
" found to read a 2022 document too, it becomes `EN22`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:132
msgid "Naming a new format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:134
msgid ""
"When a document needs a recipe of its own, rebuild its name as in the "
"first step of the heuristic, then:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:137
msgid ""
"If no format of the repository has the same **entity, language and "
"year**, that is the name. No place, no variant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:139
msgid ""
"If one does, look at whether the two documents are addressed to "
"**different places**. If they are, the place is what separates them: "
"`FINECO-EN23@LUX` and `FINECO-EN23@IR`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:141
msgid ""
"Otherwise nothing evident separates them, and the difference goes into "
"the **variant**: `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.A` and `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.B`. In the "
"repository, where a variant has been needed, every format of that entity,"
" language and year carries one, so a bare name never sits beside its own "
"variants."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:146
msgid ""
"The variant is the last resort on purpose. It is the only segment a user "
"cannot read off a document, so every variant added is one more format to "
"try blindly at step 2 of the heuristic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:149
msgid "Partial names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:151
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The same grammar, cut short, names a **set** of formats rather than one. "
"A partial name is how the repository's classification ({doc}`document-"
"classify-yaml`, {doc}`document-classify-python`) and `url_mapping.csv` "
"say *\"this much is certain\"* when they cannot say everything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "Stands for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "entity alone"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "every format of that entity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`MEDIOLANUM`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "entity and language"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "every year, place and variant under them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`MEDIOLANUM-IT`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "ending in `@*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the formats that **have** a place, any place"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`FINECO-EN23@*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "ending in `.*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "the formats that **have** a variant, any variant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`MEDIOLANUM-IT24.*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "ending in `@*.*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "both open"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:16
msgid "`X-EN23@*.*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:163
msgid "Three rules make the reading unambiguous:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:165
msgid ""
"**A name is cut only from the right.** `MEDIOLANUM-IT` is a partial name;"
" `-IT24` and `MEDIOLANUM-24` are not names at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:167
msgid ""
"**Once the year is written, a missing place or variant means *none*.** "
"`MEDIOLANUM-IT24` is a complete name, and it means the format with no "
"place and no variant. In a repository that has only `.A` and `.B`, that "
"name does not exist, and using it is an error; the set of both is written"
" `MEDIOLANUM-IT24.*`. The `@*` and `.*` endings exist so that being "
"partial is always said, never implied — which is also what lets a typo in"
" a year be caught instead of quietly becoming a set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:172
msgid ""
"**`*` is reserved.** No format may contain it, so a partial name can "
"never be mistaken for a format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/formats/names.md:175
msgid ""
"A partial name is only useful if some format of the repository falls "
"under it, and the engine checks that wherever one is written. An entity "
"containing `-` is legal in a complete name but cannot be written in a "
"partial one, because the cut would be ambiguous; none of the repository's"
" formats has one."
msgstr ""

